course: dictionary
lesson: 10196
title: Timpani: the tuned drums of the orchestra
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Timpani are big drums that can play real notes, not just beats. Each one is a large, round, bowl-shaped drum made of copper, with a skin or plastic top stretched over it. Another name for them is kettledrums.
explain: Timpani is a plural word, so one drum is a timpano, but that word is rare. You will almost always hear the word timpani. The player is called a timpanist, and they strike the top with soft-headed sticks.
explain: Context gives you a clue: «Percussion means instruments that are hit, shaken or scraped; some, like the xylophone and the timpani (big orchestra drums tuned to notes), play real notes.» The words in brackets tell you that timpani are tuned to notes.
explain: In an orchestra, the timpani sit in the percussion section at the back. The timpanist uses pedals to change the pitch, so the same drum can play a higher or lower note.
fact: Timpani are large, bowl-shaped drums made of copper.
fact: Most drums cannot be tuned to a note, but each timpano can be tuned to play one particular note.
fact: Timpani is a plural word: one drum is a timpano, but you will almost always hear timpani.
fact: Timpani are also called kettledrums, and their player is a timpanist.
fact: Timpani make rolls like thunder and dramatic booming beats, as in «Also sprach Zarathustra».
exercise: What are timpani? | Long pipes blown like a trumpet | Wooden sticks tapped together to keep a beat | Small metal bells rung by hand | *Large, bowl-shaped drums that can be tuned to notes
exercise: In «the timpani (big orchestra drums tuned to notes)», what does tuned mean? | Hit very hard | Played only at night | Covered in copper | *Set to play a particular musical note
exercise: Where do the timpani sit in an orchestra? | Up in the balcony | *At the back, in the percussion section | Hidden behind the piano | At the front, next to the violins
test: What is another name for timpani? | Xylophones | *Kettledrums | Triangles | Cymbals
test: What does a timpanist use to change the pitch? | Keys | *Pedals | Valves | Bows
exam: Which statement about timpani is true? | They are played by blowing down a tube | They have four keys like a piano | They have strings that are plucked | *They are bowl-shaped drums that can be tuned to notes
